# KernelSU/Magisk runtime sepolicy for okcar-port.
#
# Two unrelated capabilities need a rule here.  Both were measured on 217 (Android 15 / HyperOS).
#
# ── 1. the wpa_supplicant control interface (why this file existed) ─────────────
# Driving a *second* station interface (p2p0) means talking to the wpa_supplicant the framework
# already runs, over its ctrl_interface.  Android's policy lets that supplicant answer the
# framework's own clients only, so a client in the KernelSU domain gets no reply and the tool
# reports a dead socket while the supplicant is perfectly healthy:
#
#   $ wpa_cli -p /data/vendor/wifi/wpa/sockets -i <sock> status
#   Failed to connect to non-global ctrl_ifname ... error: Connection refused
#
#   avc: denied { sendto } for comm="wpa_supplicant"
#     path=/data/vendor/wifi/wpa/sockets/wpa_ctrl_4430-1
#     scontext=u:r:hal_wifi_supplicant_default:s0 tcontext=u:r:ksu:s0
#     tclass=unix_dgram_socket permissive=0
#
# The connect itself succeeds — wpa_cli's own socket is what the supplicant cannot write its reply
# to - so the dgram sendto rule is the one that matters, and the stream and reverse rules are here
# because the global ctrl interface (@android:vendor_wpa_wlan0, from the supplicant's -g argument)
# is a stream socket.
#
# ── 2. app → daemon commands (found on 217, 2026-10-03) ─────────────────────────
# The car-screen app (the desktop app, and the LSPosed enhancer module) sends its
# commands to the daemon by appending one line to the FIFO /data/local/tmp/okcar-linkd.in.  That
# FIFO is labelled shell_data_file and untrusted_app has no access to it, so every command the app
# sent was refused with EACCES while the FIFO itself looked perfectly writable (0666):
#
#   avc: denied { append } for comm="okcar.carplaypp" name="okcar-linkd.in" dev="dm-55"
#     scontext=u:r:untrusted_app_32:s0:c213,c257,c512,c768
#     tcontext=u:object_r:shell_data_file:s0 tclass=fifo_file permissive=0
#
# Without this rule the app's 「桌面 / 状态刷新 / cast」 buttons silently do nothing on Android 11+.
# The app runs in untrusted_app_32 (a plain APK with no native code of its own picks the 32-bit
# zygote here), so both domains need the rule, the `file` rules cover the config/status files it
# reads and the two files its own UI writes (theme / standalone), which also live under
# /data/local/tmp.
#
# Trade-off, stated plainly: these rules let *any* app read and write under /data/local/tmp.  That is
# what "root module ↔ ordinary app over a file in /data/local/tmp" costs, Android offers no way to
# scope a sepolicy grant to a single package without giving that app its own SELinux domain, which
# needs a platform policy build rather than a module.  The alternative would be moving the channel
# into the app's own external files dir, it stays here because the WebUI, the LSPosed enhancer
# (system_server) and the daemon already agree on /data/local/tmp.
#
# Syntax note: KernelSU parses the space-separated form and rejects the colon form
# ("ksu:unix_dgram_socket"), and one permission per line keeps this file valid for Magisk's
# magiskpolicy as well.  `ksud sepolicy check /data/local/tmp/<this file>` returns 0 (verified on
# 217 on 2026-10-03).
#
# Comments must not contain a semicolon: ksud splits the file on ',' BEFORE stripping comments, so a
# ',' inside a comment produced
#   Error: Failed to parse policy statement:  the stream and reverse rules are here
# and the whole rule file was rejected.  Measured on 217, 2026-10-03 (a file with ',' in a comment
# fails check with rc=1, the same file without it passes with rc=0).
allow hal_wifi_supplicant_default ksu unix_dgram_socket sendto
allow hal_wifi_supplicant_default ksu unix_stream_socket sendto
allow ksu hal_wifi_supplicant_default unix_dgram_socket sendto
allow ksu hal_wifi_supplicant_default unix_stream_socket sendto

allow untrusted_app shell_data_file fifo_file write
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file fifo_file append
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file fifo_file open
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file fifo_file getattr
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file fifo_file write
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file fifo_file append
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file fifo_file open
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file fifo_file getattr
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file file read
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file file write
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file file append
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file file open
allow untrusted_app shell_data_file file getattr
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file file read
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file file write
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file file append
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file file open
allow untrusted_app_32 shell_data_file file getattr

# ── 3. the okcar-carwith bridge service (A2 — 217 Android 15. 2026-10-07) ─────────
# The CarWith component (an ordinary app — untrusted_app_32 on 217) finds the root daemon binder
# with ServiceManager.getService("okcar-carwith").  A service name that is not listed in
# /system/etc/selinux/plat_service_contexts is labelled default_android_service and no app may find
# it — the lookup came back null and every step after it (prepareCast / displayId / key / touch /
# audio) never ran:
#
#   avc: denied { find } for pid=17281 uid=10317 name=okcar-carwith
#     scontext=u:r:untrusted_app_32:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:default_android_service:s0
#     tclass=service_manager permissive=0
#
# These lines declare a type that only this one service name ever uses.  The NAME -> TYPE mapping
# itself cannot live in the policy — servicemanager reads it from plat_service_contexts — so
# post-fs-data.sh copies that file and appends the mapping and bind-mounts the copy over the
# original and then loads the policy once more.  That last step is what makes servicemanager re-read
# the file: it caches it and only reopens it when the policy has been reloaded.
#
# Only the name okcar-carwith is opened up.  Widening the whole default_android_service type instead
# (allow untrusted_app default_android_service service_manager find) would let every app find every
# unlabelled service on the device — that is the A1 variant the maintainer rejected.
#
# Syntax: the space-separated statement form is the one both root solutions accept.  Measured
# 2026-10-08 — KernelSU 3.3.0 accepts "type X service_manager_type" and rejects "type X, attr;" while
# Magisk 30.7 accepts the same space form and rejects the comma form too.  On an Android version
# without untrusted_app_32 or ksu those two allow lines are reported as unknown types and skipped —
# the rest of this file still applies (verified with magiskpolicy --load/--apply/--save on lmi).
type okcar_carwith_service service_manager_type
allow untrusted_app okcar_carwith_service service_manager find
allow untrusted_app_32 okcar_carwith_service service_manager find
allow ksu okcar_carwith_service service_manager add
allow ksu okcar_carwith_service service_manager find
